    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 1 (Questions 1&ndash;6)</div>\n<p>When Gabriel Garc\u00eda M\u00e1rquez published <i>One Hundred Years of Solitude</i> in 1967, metropolitan Western critics frequently celebrated its aesthetic audacity while simultaneously misapprehending its political urgency. Captivated by yellow butterflies, ascending ascetics, and insomnia plagues, early European and North American reviews predominantly classified magical realism as an exuberant triumph of the unfettered imagination&mdash;a quaint, folkloric escape from the disenchanted sobriety of modern industrial civilization. Yet to domesticate the genre as whimsical surrealism is to commit a grave hermeneutic error. In the postcolonial landscape of Latin America, magical realism operates not as a decorative flight from reality, but as an adversarial epistemology designed to confront an unlivable historical condition: the grotesque, systemic violence of imperial exploitation, civil conflict, and state-sponsored terror.</p>\n<p>Central to Garc\u00eda M\u00e1rquez&rsquo;s technique is what Alejo Carpentier termed <i>lo real maravilloso</i>&mdash;the marvelous real. Unlike European surrealism, which deliberately juxtaposed incongruous objects to shock the bourgeois subconscious through intellectual artifice, Latin American magical realism treats the extraordinary as an organic dimension of lived communal experience. Garc\u00eda M\u00e1rquez famously achieved this by adopting the deadpan narrative voice of his maternal grandmother, recounting supernatural apparitions and celestial portents with the same unflinching matter-of-factness reserved for ordinary domestic chores. Crucially, this narrative posture is not a romantic embrace of primitive superstition over scientific reason. Rather, it challenges the totalizing hegemony of European colonial empiricism, which had historically codified indigenous, African, and mestizo worldviews as irrational nonsense while baptizing its own violent extractive logic as the apex of enlightened modernity. By collapsing the ontological boundary between mythical cosmology and socio-political history, the narrative undermines the imperial premise that Western Cartesian rationality represents the sole legitimate arbiter of human experience.</p>\n<p>The political teeth of this aesthetic emerge most sharply in the novel&rsquo;s climactic dramatization of historical erasure: the massacre of striking banana plantation workers by national troops acting on behalf of a foreign corporate conglomerate, closely mirroring the historical 1928 United Fruit Company strike in Ci\u00e9naga. After machine guns mow down thousands of defenseless men, women, and children in the municipal square, the corpses are loaded onto a silent, endless night train and dumped into the sea. When the lone survivor, Jos\u00e9 Arcadio Segundo, awakens amidst the carnage and returns to Macondo, he discovers that official history has already obliterated the event. Schoolteachers, neighbors, and magistrates unanimously insist that no strike ever took place, that the workers returned peacefully to their homes, and that Macondo remains a contented paradise. The horror is compounded not merely by the physical slaughter, but by the state&rsquo;s total monopolization of truth and discursive reality.</p>\n<p>Here, magical realism achieves its devastating allegorical potency. In an authoritarian climate where official decrees declare the real to be nonexistent, documentary realism collapses under the weight of state censorship and collective trauma. By treating the grotesque brutality of state terror as horrifyingly real while rendering the official denial as a bizarre hallucination, Garc\u00eda M\u00e1rquez inverts the epistemological hierarchy. The fantastical becomes the only narrative vehicle sufficiently expansive to register a history characterized by systemic atrocities that rationalist discourse refuses to admit. Rather than offering a tranquilizing escape from material struggle, the marvelous becomes an insurgent aesthetic that forces readers to confront the horrifying absurdities of authoritarian governance. Some contemporary postcolonial theorists caution that excessive commercialization has ossified magical realism into a commodified literary brand, catering to Western appetites for exotic otherness. Nevertheless, at its foundational core, Garc\u00eda M\u00e1rquez&rsquo;s masterpiece demonstrates that when historical memory is systematically eradicated by the powerful, the marvelous becomes the final refuge of political witness.</p>",
