    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 4 (Questions 19&ndash;24)</div>\n<p>For more than five decades, Serbian performance artist Marina Abramovi\u0107 has interrogated the foundational boundaries of Western visual art by substituting the tangible aesthetic commodity with the living, vulnerable human body. Emerging from the radical neo-avant-garde movements of the late 1960s and early 1970s, performance art arose out of a profound disillusionment with the commercial art market. By refusing to produce portable, collectible objects that could be acquired by wealthy collectors or archived in museum vaults, early performance pioneers sought the radical dematerialization of the art object. In Abramovi\u0107&rsquo;s rigorous oeuvre, this dematerialization is not an abstract intellectual gesture, but an agonizing somatic ordeal. The artist&rsquo;s physical body becomes both canvas and chisel, subjected to physical exhaustion, acute physiological pain, and mortal peril to test the limits of consciousness and interpersonal connection.</p>\n<p>The terrifying ethical stakes of this practice were laid bare in Abramovi\u0107&rsquo;s notorious 1974 piece, <i>Rhythm 0</i>, staged in Naples. Abramovi\u0107 stood completely passive in a gallery for six hours alongside a table bearing seventy-two objects, ranging from benign items like feathers, honey, and flowers to lethal instruments including scalpels, iron chains, and a loaded pistol. A placard instructed gallery visitors that Abramovi\u0107 accepted total responsibility for anything done to her body during the performance. Initially hesitant, the audience&rsquo;s behavior steadily deteriorated into chilling sadism. Spectators cut off her clothing, slashed her skin with thorns and razor blades, and eventually pressed the loaded firearm against her temple with her own finger placed upon the trigger, prompting a brawl among attendees before the gallery ended the work. <i>Rhythm 0</i> shattered the comforting bourgeois myth of the detached, benign museum spectator, exposing how rapidly civilized social restraints collapse when institutional accountability is stripped away and the human subject is rendered an object of unrestrained power.</p>\n<p>While early works investigated physical violation and aggression, Abramovi\u0107&rsquo;s monumental 2010 retrospective at the Museum of Modern Art, <i>The Artist Is Present</i>, explored an equally grueling psychic terrain through stillness and prolonged endurance. For 736 hours over three months, Abramovi\u0107 sat motionless in a wooden chair under harsh gallery floodlights, maintaining continuous, silent eye contact with individual museum visitors who queued for hours to sit across from her. Stripped of spoken language, narrative, and theatrical movement, the performance transformed the traditionally voyeuristic museum encounter into an intense, reciprocal communion. Confronted by Abramovi\u0107&rsquo;s unblinking, nonjudgmental gaze, hundreds of participants wept openly, projecting their grief, loneliness, and latent spiritual yearning onto the silent void of the artist&rsquo;s face. Here, the traditional subject-object dichotomy of visual art dissolved; the spectator was no longer an external consumer of an aesthetic spectacle, but an active, vulnerable co-creator of the work&rsquo;s emotional architecture.</p>\n<p>Yet the enormous international celebrity generated by <i>The Artist Is Present</i> has sparked intense debate among contemporary art theorists. Skeptics argue that the spectacle of weeping visitors, magnified by viral digital media and celebrity endorsements, betrayed the anti-capitalist ethos of early performance art. By institutionalizing her practice within prestigious cultural establishments and licensing re-performances by younger artists, Abramovi\u0107 has arguably transformed her once-ephemeral body into a luxury brand and museum attraction. Nevertheless, to dismiss her work as mere narcissism is to miss its radical phenomenological core. By relentlessly anchoring art in the durational flesh, Abramovi\u0107 insists that authentic aesthetic experience cannot be simulated or commodified; it must be endured in the precarious, irreversible immediacy of real time.</p>",
