        "e": "<strong>The author contrasts Carlyle's biographical 'Great Man' theory with the Annales School's structural history (especially Braudel's tripartite time and the longue dur\u00e9e), analyzes how this reframes historical events like Lepanto, and concludes by evaluating the limits of structural determinism versus human agency. Choice B captures this comprehensive purpose.</strong><br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A (Directional Inversion): The passage shows that Carlyle's paradigm was decisively dismantled by twentieth-century social history rather than defending it.<br>&bull; C (Directional Inversion): Braudel reconceptualized Lepanto as a fleeting event with virtually no lasting structural impact on Mediterranean trade.<br>&bull; D (Directional Inversion): The passage emphasizes the immense importance of geographical topography in Braudel's framework, rather than claiming it is irrelevant."
